-<p>Let the number be divided by 65,537, and let its remainder be the <i>multiplier</i>. Let any number from 1 to 65,536 be multiplied by it and divided again by 65,537, and let the remainder be that number&rsquo;s successor; let the successor be treated the same way, and so on. This is the rule of the ZX Spectrum, whose manual gives the multiplier 75 and the divisor 65,537, and whose <span class="sc">RND</span> is this rule, shifted by one, divided down to a fraction.<sup><a href="#n1">1</a></sup> It is the plainest of the <i>multiplicative congruential</i> rules, for decades the commonest way for a computer to make chance.</p>
+<p>Let the number be divided by 65,537, and let its remainder be the <a href="/definitions/#d10"><i>multiplier</i></a>. Let any number from 1 to 65,536 be multiplied by it and divided again by 65,537, and let the remainder be that number&rsquo;s successor; let the successor be treated the same way, and so on. This is the rule of the ZX Spectrum, whose manual gives the multiplier 75 and the divisor 65,537, and whose <span class="sc">RND</span> is this rule, shifted by one, divided down to a fraction.<sup><a href="#n1">1</a></sup> It is the plainest of the <i>multiplicative congruential</i> rules, for decades the commonest way for a computer to make chance.</p>
 <p>Let a square be ruled, and for every number let a dot be set with the number across and its successor up. Fig. 1 lays the dots down in the order the rule gives them, starting from 1, so that the square fills as the machine runs. Fig. 2 takes the middle of the square and magnifies it until single dots can be told apart; the grey lines are drawn through them along the shortest step from one dot to another, and the shortest step is drawn in solid blue, and the shortest that does not run the same way in broken blue. Fig. 1 is drawn at the screen&rsquo;s own pixels, each as dark as the dots that fall in it; where the rows are finer than a pixel, the eye sees only a grey.</p>
 
