 <h2>Construction</h2>
 <p>Let the minute be read as a number of four figures, the hour and then the minutes, so that a quarter to four in the afternoon is 1545. Let it be squared, the square written out in eight figures with noughts in front if it is short, and the middle four kept. Thus 1545 squared is 02387025, and the middle four are 3870. Let the same be done to 3870, and so on, until a number comes that has come before. This is the <i>middle-square</i> rule, which John von Neumann put forward in 1949 as a way for a computing machine to make its own random digits.<sup><a href="#n1">1</a></sup></p>
-<p>Let a dial be drawn for the day, midnight at the top and noon at the bottom, and let each of its 1,440 minutes put out a spoke, as long as the number of steps that minute takes before it falls into a run that repeats: a <i>round</i>. Let the spokes of minutes that fall to nought, after which the rule gives nothing but nought, be grey. Fig. 2 follows the given minute&rsquo;s run step by step, the height of each point being the number reached, in blue while it wanders and in ink once the round has begun.</p>
+<p>Let a dial be drawn for the day, midnight at the top and noon at the bottom, and let each of its 1,440 minutes put out a spoke, as long as the number of steps that minute takes before it falls into a run that repeats: a <a href="/definitions/#d9"><i>round</i></a>. Let the spokes of minutes that fall to nought, after which the rule gives nothing but nought, be grey. Fig. 2 follows the given minute&rsquo;s run step by step, the height of each point being the number reached, in blue while it wanders and in ink once the round has begun.</p>
 <p>Von Neumann himself worked with numbers of many more figures. Four figures are taken here because a clock shows four, and because ten thousand numbers can be followed to the end.<sup><a href="#n1">1</a></sup></p>
 
