 <h2>Construction</h2>
-<p>Let a grid of squares be ruled, and let every grain fall on the middle square. Whenever a square holds four grains or more, let it give one grain to each of its four neighbours, and let this go on until no square holds four. Each such giving is a <i>toppling</i>, and the topplings one new grain sets going are its <i>avalanche</i>. This is the sandpile of Bak, Tang and Wiesenfeld.<sup><a href="#n1">1</a></sup></p>
+<p>Let a grid of squares be ruled, and let every grain fall on the middle square. Whenever a square holds four grains or more, let it give one grain to each of its four neighbours, and let this go on until no square holds four. Each such giving is a <a href="/definitions/#d4"><i>toppling</i></a>, and the topplings one new grain sets going are its <a href="/definitions/#d5"><i>avalanche</i></a>. This is the sandpile of Bak, Tang and Wiesenfeld.<sup><a href="#n1">1</a></sup></p>
 <p>Let the grains be counted by the calendar: one for the first of January 1970, and one more for each day after it, reckoned in Greenwich time. The figure shows the pile at rest once the day&rsquo;s grain has fallen, with the squares that grain toppled marked in blue. On a day whose grain topples nothing, let the next days&rsquo; grains be dropped in advance until one of them does, and let the squares that grain will topple be outlined in blue. An avalanche too small to see at this scale is ringed.</p>
 
