 <h2>Construction</h2>
-<p>Let the word be stripped to its letters, at most twelve, and let each letter stand for one shade, from the bare ground for the first letter to full ink for the last. Let every square of a grid 257 squares on a side begin in the first shade, and let an ant stand on the middle square facing up.</p>
+<p>Let the word be stripped to its letters, at most twelve, and let each letter stand for one shade, from the bare ground for the first letter to full ink for the last. Let every square of a grid 257 squares on a side begin in the first shade, and let an <a href="/definitions/#d7">ant</a> stand on the middle square facing up.</p>
 <p>At each step the ant reads the shade of its square. If that shade&rsquo;s letter is a vowel, it turns a quarter left; if a consonant, a quarter right. It then moves the square on to the next shade, the last shade returning to the first, and steps forward one square. The grid is closed on itself, so an ant that walks off one edge comes back on the opposite one. Let it take 400,000 steps. The figure shows the smallest square of grid that holds everything the ant has reached so far.</p>
 <p>With the word <i>la</i>, a consonant and then a vowel, this is Langton&rsquo;s ant;<sup><a href="#n1">1</a></sup> longer words give the many-coloured ants of Turk and Propp.<sup><a href="#n2">2</a></sup></p>
 
